.TH ERASE 8
.SH NAME
erase \- tell the SD card a partition is empty
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBerase\fR [\fB\-q\fR] \fI/dev/hdN\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Erase
sends the SD ERASE command (CMD32, CMD33, CMD38) over a whole partition
of the SD card, so the card knows that all of it is empty.
A card that was once full (from a camera or a phone) then has its whole
free pool for wear levelling again (Galtzagorriak MINIX, spec R12.33).
Everything on the partition is gone: make a file system on it with
.BR mkfs (1)
afterwards, or use it for swap.
.PP
.B /etc/setup
runs it over
.BR /dev/hd2 " (" /usr ),
.BR /dev/hd3 " (swap) and"
.BR /dev/hd4 " (" /home )
on the first boot, before
.BR mkfs ,
with the line "Preparing the card..." on the screen.
It is an install-time step: an erase can take seconds, and on a cheap
card it may start the card's garbage collection, so the system never
erases at run time.
.PP
Only root may erase (the
.B DIOCERASE
ioctl on the partition device).
.B Erase
refuses the whole card
.RB ( /dev/hd0 ),
a partition that is mounted or holds a mounted subpartition, and the swap
partition while swap is on, with
.BR EBUSY .
.TP
.B \-q
say nothing when the card or the driver cannot erase; only the exit
status 2 tells.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 erased, 1 an error, 2 the card or the driver cannot erase.
.SH NOTES
On the Flipper the SD driver cannot erase yet (task T18b):
.B erase
says "this card or driver cannot erase" and exits with 2, and setup goes
on without it.
In QEMU the disk image has no ERASE: the range gets the erased value,
0x00.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mkfs (1).
